The presenter's waits for the driver have a limit (Granite's WSI)

DW5XL issue 049, amendment v3.13 (2026-11-15). The thread that presents is the window's message loop. With vsync
on, WSI::begin_frame() waits for an earlier present to have been shown (wait_swapchain_latency ->
vkWaitForPresent2KHR) and for a free swapchain image (vkAcquireNextImageKHR), both without a time limit. On
2026-11-15 the driver never answered the first of them after a vsynced swapchain had been made anew, and the
window stopped for good; a dark screen ends in the same wait.

WSI::set_wait_limit(ns): the longest either wait lasts, and the waits for a swapchain's last presents in
drain_swapchain() with it. UINT64_MAX (the default) keeps the library's own behaviour. A wait of begin_frame()
that ran into the limit is noted for the caller (take_wait_timeout): begin_frame() returns false when there was
no image, and goes on with the frame when it was the wait for an earlier present. request_new_swapchain() has
the swapchain made anew at the next begin_frame(), as after a resize, without waiting for the old one's presents.
set_wait_test_fault() is the tests' silent driver: waits that last the limit and are noted.

This is the first patch to a file of the Granite submodule: `git -C <parallel-gs checkout> apply` applies it as
the others.

Applies on top of 0001-0007.

diff --git a/Granite/vulkan/wsi.cpp b/Granite/vulkan/wsi.cpp
--- a/Granite/vulkan/wsi.cpp
+++ b/Granite/vulkan/wsi.cpp
@@ -76,6 +76,46 @@
 	present_frame_latency = latency;
 }
 
+// DW5XL (issue 049, amendment v3.13): no wait for the driver without a limit. See wsi.hpp.
+void WSI::set_wait_limit(uint64_t nanoseconds)
+{
+	wait_limit_ns = nanoseconds ? nanoseconds : UINT64_MAX;
+}
+
+bool WSI::take_wait_timeout(WaitTimeout &timeout)
+{
+	if (wait_timeout.kind == WaitKind::None)
+		return false;
+	timeout = wait_timeout;
+	wait_timeout = {};
+	return true;
+}
+
+void WSI::request_new_swapchain()
+{
+	// begin_frame() makes a swapchain anew when the current one is suboptimal, without blocking on the old one.
+	swapchain_is_suboptimal = true;
+}
+
+void WSI::set_wait_test_fault(WaitKind kind, uint64_t after_frames, uint32_t count)
+{
+	wait_fault_kind = kind;
+	wait_fault_after_frames = after_frames;
+	wait_fault_count = count;
+}
+
+// The test's silent driver: true when this wait gets no answer (it has lasted the limit and is noted).
+bool WSI::wait_test_fault(WaitKind kind, uint64_t present_id)
+{
+	if (kind != wait_fault_kind || !wait_fault_now || wait_fault_count == 0)
+		return false;
+	wait_fault_count--;
+	const uint64_t waited = wait_limit_ns != UINT64_MAX ? wait_limit_ns : 1000000000ull;
+	std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::nanoseconds(waited));
+	wait_timeout = { kind, present_id, waited };
+	return true;
+}
